--TEST--
A `foreach` binding declares the type of every value it reads, and a binding that disagrees with the subject is a compile error over an array and over an `Iterator<T>` alike — `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:iteration/what-foreach-walks
class Steps {
    public static function upto(int $n): Iterator<int> {
        int $i = 0;
        while ($i < $n) {
            yield $i;
            $i = $i + 1;
        }
    }
}

// The array holds `string`, so binding its values as `int` is refused at the binding.
array<string> $names = ["Ada", "Bob"];
foreach ($names as int $n) {
    echo $n, "\n";
}

// The cursor gives `int`, so binding its values as `string` is refused the same way: the
// check reaches the cursor forms and not the array form alone.
foreach (Steps::upto(3) as string $s) {
    echo $s, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `int`, found `string`
%A
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `int`
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
